      "prompt": "For the same honey bottle, photorealism sells “rarity”; stylized 3D sells “approachability.”\n🍯 photoreal glass jar, thick amber honey stream, translucent honeycomb cells, real bees kept small\nThis makes the viewer believe in the honey’s weight, viscosity, and value first. In a realistic style, the honeycomb, glass, and flowing honey must behave like real materials; any character should stay secondary, otherwise the image quickly turns into a children’s ad.\n✨ stylized mascot bee beside the jar, rounded friendly proportions, simplified honey glow, softer jungle depth\nThis solves the entry point for stylized 3D: it relies less on material pressure and more on emotional access. The image feels lighter and more friendly, but the luxury-food rarity becomes weaker, so this direction is better for brand storytelling than for selling rare material quality.\n\nGPT- image 2 prompt👇\nCreate an ultra-premium flagship honey advertising poster in a polished stylized 3D realism style, preserving the exact core visual logic of an open hero honey bottle placed beneath a suspended wild honeycomb in a radiant mangrove forest sanctuary. The image must feel luxurious, sacred, naturally rare, visually delicious, and Cannes-level premium, with the bottle as the absolute visual leader and the honey as the emotional heat core of the entire composition.\n\nUse a tall vertical composition. Center one original premium honey bottle in the lower-middle foreground on an elevated ceremonial wooden harvest altar carved from a rich tropical tree cross-section. The altar must feel more iconic and brand-specific than a normal tabletop, with visible growth rings, engraved edge motifs, subtle symbolic carving, moss traces, and a sense of ritual craftsmanship. The bottle must have a sculpted transparent body filled with deep amber honey, a refined dark label, and elegant fictional branding such as “MANGORA GOLD” with a smaller subtitle like “Wild Mangrove Reserve Honey.” The label must feel luxurious, readable, and premium. Important: the bottle cap must be opened and removed, placed beside the bottle on the wooden altar, clearly visible as a separate premium object. The bottle mouth must remain open so the honey stream reads as actively flowing into the product.\n\nAbove the bottle, suspend a large glowing wild honeycomb from a thick mangrove branch. The honeycomb must be larger, more imposing, and more visually sacred, with exquisite hexagonal wax cells, darker honey chambers, subtle translucency, and heavy natural sag. From the lowest point of the comb, a thick, slow, highly viscous stream of honey must pour directly downward into the open bottle mouth, forming a powerful vertical golden axis. The stream must feel denser, richer, heavier, and more luxurious than ordinary honey, like liquid amber or molten wild gold.\n\nThe honey color must be pushed stronger and more expensive: radiant imperial honey-gold, deep amber, saffron gold, sunlit resin, and nectar bronze. It should glow with precious richness, catching concentrated highlights and feeling like a rare harvested treasure rather than a common food product. The gold of the honey must dominate the emotional center of the image and read as the most valuable substance in the scene.\n\nSet the environment in a lush mangrove forest sanctuary with exposed roots, humid air, glossy leaves, warm shafts of sunlight, and controlled atmospheric mist rising behind the bottle. Add only a few small real bees around the comb and stream. Include selective tropical leaves and blossoms at the edges for richness, but keep the composition highly controlled so the bottle, open cap, and honey flow remain dominant. The whole scene should feel like a sacred point of origin deep inside a wild tidal forest.\n\nLighting must be cinematic and highly refined. Use strong golden sunlight from upper left and upper rear, sending warm god rays through the canopy and through the honeycomb. The honey itself must act like a precious luminous core, glowing more intensely than before and casting rich amber reflections onto the bottle rim, glass shoulders, label edges, altar surface, and nearby leaves. Add soft jungle fill so the bottle silhouette, open cap, label, glass refraction, and honey reflections remain perfectly legible. The final light must feel pure, rich, and luxurious, never muddy.\n\nRender all materials with exceptional realism: thick clear bottle walls with precise refraction, dense high-viscosity honey with realistic cling and hanging tension, semi-translucent wax comb, damp bark texture, glossy leaves, fine bee-wing translucency, and a subtly humid forest atmosphere. Let a small controlled pool of honey gather around the bottle base or altar surface for appetizing realism, but keep it clean and premium.\n\nPreserve the poster logic of bold bottom typography, but use only fully original fictional copy. Add an elegant headline at the bottom, such as “MANGORA GOLD,” with a smaller premium line beneath like “RARE WILD NECTAR OF THE TIDAL FOREST.” The text must be luxurious, sparse, fully original, and compositionally integrated.\n\nColor palette: imperial honey gold, deep amber, saffron glow, nectar bronze, mangrove green, wet bark brown, warm cream light, and subtle glass highlights. Keep the honey gold as the unmistakable visual monarch of the poster.\n\nThe final image must feel like a world-class flagship honey campaign: product-dominant, rare, sacred, naturally luxurious, and unforgettable.\n\nNegative prompt:\nreal brand names, copied packaging text, copied headline text, watermark, signature, plastic bottle, fake honey, weak honey stream, closed bottle cap, missing cap, cap still attached, muddy shadows, black blotches, cheap cartoon look, cluttered foliage, low-detail bees, unreadable label, low resolution, messy composition",
