      "prompt": "Risograph collage travel posters\n\n GPT Image 2\n\nPrompt:\nFORMAT. 3:4 vertical travel poster - handmade risograph screenprint and cut-paper collage, scanned from physical paper.\n\nPRINT. Warm cream fiber paper (#F1E7D6), visible paper tooth and one subtle fold crease. Paper is the lightest value; no white ink. Use three plates only: black, pale sea teal, and coral. Plate overlaps create the only additional hues. Keep black coverage under 35%.\n\nTYPE. [CITY LANDMARK / TITLE], ultra-condensed heavy grotesque uppercase lettering, arranged in two stacked lines bleeding slightly past both side edges and filling approximately 65% of the frame height. The second line is wider than the first. Black lettering with a 3mm coral misregistration ghost.\n\nFIGURE. Young adult woman, early twenties, wind-lifted hair, standing in the defining pedestrian/public area of the destination. Three-quarter view, turned back over her shoulder, with one hand holding her hair away from her face. She stands in front of the typography and partially covers it — letterforms are cut off at the figure, while the top third of every letter remains clear. Skin rendered as dense black halftone photo-transfer with varying dot density and visible paper showing through the highlights. Hand-cut paper silhouette with a 4mm bare-paper margin and a thin teal offset outline.\n\nCOLLAGE FILLS. Clothing and accessories use glued-paper textures with torn edges. Jacket or windbreaker: coral cross-hatch weave. Trousers: pale teal cloud-print. Add a distinctive local visual fragment behind her shoulder, plus a torn kraft-paper scrap attached to or near the bag.\n\nEYE BAND. Flat semi-opaque coral rectangle across her eyes, slightly wider than her face. Eyes remain visible underneath as dark halftone. Edges should be slightly imperfect and physically printed.\n\nBACKGROUND. One instantly recognizable architectural or structural landmark placed left of centre. Signature lines, cables, streets, coastlines, or structural elements fan outward toward both edges as thin black lines. The destination's recognizable background environment appears far behind in flat teal halftone. Foreground surfaces, paths, streets, steps, or architectural lines converge naturally across the bottom.\n\nACCENTS. Add a coral drybrush band low on the horizon and a teal diagonal band in the upper-left area. Include small destination-specific environmental silhouettes in the upper-right and lower-left corners. Mid-right on the coral band, place two lines of small lowercase marker handwriting: \"[SHORT TRAVEL NOTE LINE 1] / [SHORT TRAVEL NOTE LINE 2]\".\n\nSURFACE. Ink visibly sinking into paper fibre, authentic plate misregistration, physically cut paper edges, faint scan noise, matte printed finish, subtle handmade imperfections. Maintain an even 8% margin on all four sides.\n\nAVOID. Photorealistic skin, glossy or vector-smooth finish, 3D shading, painterly rendering, neon glow, gradients. No signature, watermark, or edition number. No extra people, signage, logos, or doodle lines. The main title and handwritten note are the only text elements.",
      "prompt_ko": "★★**색을 «고르는» 대신 «판을 몇 장 쓸지»로 지정한다.**\n```\n★three plates only: black · pale sea teal · coral\n★Plate overlaps create the only additional hues   ← 나머지 색은 «겹쳐서» 만든다\n★Paper is the lightest value; ★no white ink       ← 흰색은 «잉크»가 아니라 «종이»\n```\n리소그래프의 물리 조건을 그대로 옮긴 것이고, 이 세 줄이 «리소풍»이라는 형용사보다 훨씬 정확하게 그 인쇄를 소환한다.\n\n★★**인물과 타이포의 관계를 «잘라 먹기»로 규정했다** — *「인물이 타이포 앞에 서서 일부를 가린다. 글자는 인물에서 잘리되 **모든 글자의 위 1/3은 그대로 남는다**」*. 가리는 양을 «비율»로 못박아서 제목이 여전히 읽힌다. 오늘 배치12에서 얻은 「겹침은 누가 가리는지를 적어야 정해진다」와 같은 문법이다.\n\n★**미스레지스터를 «3mm 코럴 고스트»로 수치화했다** — 「살짝 어긋난 느낌」이 아니라 «어느 색이 얼마나 밀렸는지»를 적는다.\n\n★피부를 «검정 하프톤 사진 전사»로, 옷을 «찢은 종이 텍스처»로 나눠 한 인물 안에서 두 재료가 부딪치게 했다. ⚠️원문 예시가 여성이라 남성으로 채웠다(런북 §슬롯 기본값).",
      "gen_prompt": "A handmade risograph screenprint and cut-paper collage travel poster, vertical, scanned from physical paper. The paper is a warm cream fibre stock with visible tooth and one subtle fold crease, and it is the lightest value in the image — there is no white ink. Only three plates are printed: black, pale sea teal and coral, and the overlaps between them create the only additional hues, with black coverage kept low. The title, HONG KONG, is set in ultra-condensed heavy grotesque uppercase, arranged in two stacked lines that bleed slightly past both side edges and fill about two thirds of the frame height, the second line wider than the first, printed in black with a three-millimetre coral misregistration ghost beside it. A young man in a windbreaker stands in front of the typography and partially covers it — the letterforms are cut off where he stands while the top third of every letter stays clear — seen in three-quarter view, turned back over his shoulder, one hand pushing his wind-lifted hair off his forehead. His skin is rendered as dense black halftone photo-transfer with varying dot density and the paper showing through the highlights, and he is a hand-cut paper silhouette with a bare-paper margin and a thin teal offset outline. His clothing uses glued-paper textures with torn edges: the windbreaker a coral cross-hatch weave, the trousers a pale teal cloud print, with a torn kraft-paper scrap attached near his bag. A flat semi-opaque coral rectangle lies across his eyes, slightly wider than his face, his eyes still visible underneath as dark halftone, its edges slightly imperfect and physically printed. Behind him, left of centre, stands a tall terraced harbour tower, with tram cables and street lines fanning outward toward both edges as thin black lines and the hills and harbour far behind in flat teal halftone; foreground steps and street lines converge across the bottom. A coral drybrush band sits low on the horizon and a teal diagonal band crosses the upper left, with small skyline silhouettes in the upper-right and lower-left corners. On the coral band at mid right, two lines of small lowercase marker handwriting read ferry at eight / then the peak. Ink sinks visibly into the paper fibre with authentic plate misregistration, physically cut paper edges, faint scan noise and a matte printed finish, with an even margin on all four sides. The title and that handwritten note are the only text elements.",
