      "prompt": "The trick is not splitting photo and blueprint.\nIt is making one become the other.\nThe villa works because the built side and the linework side share the same roof edges, glass rhythm, stair logic, and structural grid.\n✨ the finished villa gradually dissolves into precise architectural section linework\nThis keeps the transition from feeling like a collage. You see the solid house thin out into drawing lines, while the same architecture remains readable across both sides.\n🧊 three dominant symbols only: real villa, technical drawing, architect hand\nThis removes the usual sales-poster noise. The viewer understands the whole idea instantly: design, construction, and luxury living are part of one controlled process.\n\nGPT- image 2 prompt👇\nCreate an ultra-premium architectural sales poster for a fictional luxury real estate developer, designed as a concept-to-reality hero visual where a finished villa appears to actively grow out of its own architectural drawing. Preserve the exact core composition: a clean white background, a modern residence centered in frontal view, the left side rendered as a fully realized hyper-real built architecture photograph, the right side rendered as an elegant black technical drawing and section-elevation sketch, with a realistic hand holding a black pen in the lower-right foreground. The entire image must feel like a world-class developer launch campaign, an architect’s design statement, and a collectible premium sales poster at the same time.\n\nThe most important visual force is the transition energy from left to right: the real built architecture must appear to extend, dissolve, and transform into drafting lines, as if the building is actively emerging from the blueprint. Strengthen this transformation so the slab edges, glazing lines, roof plane, stair geometry, structural grid, and interior partitions all flow progressively from fully built form on the left into semi-material linework and then into pure architectural drawing on the right. The transition must feel elegant, deliberate, and fluid, never abrupt or collage-like. It should look as though the villa is being born from the architect’s pen and technical intelligence.\n\nThe building itself is the absolute visual protagonist: a refined two-story contemporary villa with crisp white volumes, floor-to-ceiling low-iron glass, thin black or dark bronze framing, warm timber soffits, calm horizontal slabs, and a stepped landscape plinth. The left built side must feel highly desirable and fully real, with luminous glazing, soft warm interiors, pale plaster or stone walls, elegant furniture silhouettes, subtle reflections, restrained landscaping, and a few trees placed to support the composition. The architecture must remain believable, buildable, luxurious, and globally premium.\n\nOn the right side, depict the same house as a precise architectural drawing using premium black technical linework: section cuts, elevation lines, dimensional strings, structural notation, staircase indications, interior outlines, construction guides, and delicate drafting hierarchy. The linework must be rigorous but beautiful, standardized yet poetic, with enough information to feel like a real architect’s presentation, but kept clean and elegant. The drawing density should gradually increase toward the far right while remaining airy and highly legible.\n\nPlace a realistic adult hand in the lower-right foreground holding a black pen, naturally aligned with the drawing process. The hand must be anatomically correct and secondary to the architecture, but it should reinforce the idea that design is actively generating reality. Fingers must have correct proportions, realistic joints, believable skin texture, natural grip tension, and no deformation. The pen tip should connect organically to the drafted line system.\n\nPush the Transit direction strongly: simplify all graphic noise, reduce annotation clutter, enlarge negative space, refine alignment, and make the entire image feel more international, more luxurious, more editorial, and more controlled. Push the Port direction strongly: compress the full visual memory into three dominant symbols only, the left real villa, the right technical drawing, and the drawing hand in the lower-right. Everything else must stay subordinate. The image should feel cleaner, sharper, and more brand-defining than a typical architecture board.\n\nLighting should be soft, premium, and controlled. The left built architecture should be washed in bright elegant daylight with gentle shadow depth and warm interior glow visible through the glass. The right drawing side should remain lighter, flatter, and more graphic, but still physically coherent within the same visual world. Increase the tonal separation between real architecture and drawing just enough to heighten the transformation effect. Keep the whole page airy, luminous, and free of heavy black masses.\n\nGraphic language must be extremely clean and high-end: large negative space, thin black drafting lines, refined micro-annotation marks, minimal fictional English project title and discreet developer logo, and elegant editorial spacing. Use only original invented English naming. Typography must be sparse, luxurious, and secondary to the architectural image. The poster should feel like a flagship launch ad for a signature villa product.\n\nMaterial rendering must be exceptionally refined: matte white walls, clear low-iron glass, warm natural wood soffits, pale stone terraces, precise mullions, subtle landscape textures, soft ground shadow, crisp white presentation surface, and premium black ink drafting lines. The overall fusion of built architecture and design representation must feel seamless, intelligent, and immediately striking.\n\nColor system: dominant white and soft warm gray, black technical lines, pale greenery, amber timber warmth, soft golden interior glow, and minimal neutral landscape tones. Keep saturation restrained, premium, and globally architectural.\n\nNo copied project names, no real company names, no Chinese headline, no cluttered annotations, no messy sketch lines, no abrupt split between photo and drawing, no distorted perspective, no fantasy structure, no extra buildings, no muddy shadows, no black blotches, no watermark, no existing brand logos, no malformed hands, no extra fingers, no broken finger anatomy.",
      "prompt_ko": "★★**작성자가 먼저 적은 한 줄이 전부다.**\n```\n★\"요령은 사진과 청사진을 «나누는» 것이 아니다. ★하나가 다른 하나가 «되게» 하는 것이다\"\n```\n좌우로 갈라 붙이면 두 장의 그림이고, **같은 창문이 절반은 도면이고 절반은 실물**이면 한 장의 전환이 된다. 오늘 반복 확인한 「경계를 넘는 것 하나가 화면을 묶는다」의 건축판이다.\n\n★**같은 부재로 이어 붙인 것이 핵심** — 같은 창, 같은 난간이 «선으로 시작해 브러시드 스틸로 끝난다». 다른 부위를 각각 도면/사진으로 두면 전환이 안 읽힌다.",
      "gen_prompt": "An architectural sales poster for a fictional luxury developer, built as a concept-to-reality transition. A modern hillside villa is shown once, but across the sheet it changes state: at the left edge it is a precise blue-line drafting drawing on white — elevation lines, dimension arrows, hatch patterns — and moving right the drawn lines thicken, gain material and resolve into a fully photographic building with real concrete, glass reflections and planted terraces at the right edge. There is no seam or divider between the two states: the same window is half drafted and half built, the same railing starts as a line and ends as brushed steel. Below, a slim band carries small technical annotations and a plan fragment. Cool white ground, drafting blue, and the warm greys of the built material. A short line of type at the top reads TERRA HOUSE and a smaller line beneath it reads CONCEPT TO REALITY. Those two lines are the only text in the image.",
