      "prompt": "The smartest part of this board isn’t the houses.\nIt’s the upward product logic.\nEach module gets lighter, more open, and more premium as it rises, so the viewer understands the whole real estate system before reading any label.\n🪽 a rising diagonal sequence of modular residences, each unit slightly lifted above the last\nThis makes the product hierarchy visible immediately: entry model at the bottom, flagship model at the top.\n🧊 clean white presentation background, ultra-light technical callout lines, large negative space\nThis keeps the board from feeling crowded. The architecture becomes the hero, while the labels feel precise, quiet, and premium.\n\nGPT- image 2 prompt👇\nCreate an ultra-premium vertical architectural sales poster for a fictional luxury real estate developer, presenting a modular residential product family through a highly refined exploded-axonometric composition with a clear upward serialized progression. Preserve the original visual logic of a clean white presentation background, large negative space, precise technical linework, minimal English annotations, and a world-class premium brand-board aesthetic. The entire image must immediately communicate one core idea: the product system rises upward in a structured series, from grounded entry model to lighter, more open, more elevated flagship modules.\n\nMain composition: arrange five to six modular residential units in a strong rising diagonal sequence from lower right to upper left, with each successive module slightly lifted, lighter, more transparent, and more architecturally advanced than the one below. The sequence must feel like a product line ascending in value, openness, and sophistication. The lowest unit should read as the most compact and grounded version; the middle units should progressively open with more glass, deck extensions, and planted zones; the uppermost unit should feel like the most refined, airy, premium culmination of the series. The entire composition must create an unmistakable “upward product family” momentum at first glance.\n\nArchitecture style: contemporary minimalist modular residences with slender dark metal frames, floor-to-ceiling low-iron glass, warm timber cladding, pale concrete bases, clean roof planes, integrated terraces, and subtle landscape pockets. Every module must remain fully believable and buildable. Maintain a unified family DNA across all units so they read as one branded system, but clearly differentiate their degree of openness, landscaping, enclosure, and luxury level to reinforce the serialized hierarchy.\n\nPush the Transit direction strongly: reduce all unnecessary graphic density, increase negative space, refine alignment, lighten annotation noise, and make the whole board feel more international, more editorial, more luxurious, and more controlled. Push the Port direction strongly: compress the page memory into three dominant super symbols only, the rising diagonal module series, the surrounding ultra-light technical callout system, and the minimal brand/title zone. Everything else must remain subordinate.\n\nSurround the module sequence with elegant thin English callout lines, tiny icon markers, subtle drafting grids, and sparse premium technical labels placed mainly on the left and right sides. Add a small minimalist fictional developer logo and one refined English project title near the upper area, using original invented naming only. Typography should be architectural, understated, and secondary to the product system.\n\nLighting and rendering: soft pale daylight or overcast studio-environment daylight with extremely controlled shadows. Each module should cast a delicate floating shadow onto the white ground plane to emphasize separation and hierarchy. Interior lighting should glow softly in selected units, revealing warm domestic atmosphere, timber ceilings, shelving, seating, and pendant lights without clutter. Exterior materials must feel precise and tactile: matte dark metal, clear glass, warm oak or cedar, pale concrete, fine paving, and realistic controlled vegetation.\n\nLandscape integration: each unit should include subtle premium micro-landscape conditions such as low shrubs, small trees, planted decks, gravel strips, and pale paving grids. Vegetation should feel increasingly curated and elevated as the series moves upward, reinforcing the sense of product evolution rather than random decoration. The white background must remain dominant, with only faint gray grid traces, site hints, and drawing overlays.\n\nGraphic style: world-class real estate developer product launch board, elite modular housing campaign, museum-clean architectural presentation, collectible editorial layout, axonometric precision, calm but powerful branding. The modules should feel simultaneously like real residences and like beautifully organized product data. The page must feel pristine, aspirational, and immediately legible as a premium serialized housing system.\n\nColor system: white and warm gray base, charcoal-dark frames, honey timber, pale concrete, muted green and ochre vegetation, soft warm interior amber, and subtle gray drafting overlays. Keep saturation restrained, premium, and globally architectural. Strengthen depth with clean shadow hierarchy and slightly warmer interior glow, but keep the page airy and minimal.\n\nNo copied project names, no real company names, no Chinese headline, no cluttered annotations, no fantasy impossible structure, no distorted axonometric perspective, no muddy shadows, no black blotches, no excessive furniture, no messy landscape, no random people, no watermark, no existing brand logos.",
      "prompt_ko": "★**작성자의 한 줄** — *「이 보드에서 가장 영리한 부분은 «집»이 아니다. ★«위로 향하는 제품 논리»다. 각 모듈이 위로 갈수록 더 가볍고, 더 개방적이고, 더 비싸진다」*.\n\n★**같은 평면 위에서 «무게와 개방도»만 바뀌게 못박은 것이 요령이다** — 위치가 바뀌면 다른 건물이 되고, 무게만 바뀌면 «한 시스템의 등급»이 된다.\n\n★조적 → 철골 → 전면 유리 → 지붕면만 남은 퍼걸러. **재료의 계단이 곧 가격표다.**",
      "gen_prompt": "A vertical architectural sales poster for a fictional luxury developer presenting a modular residential system, arranged as a stack of tiers that reads upward. At the base sit heavy solid modules — board-marked concrete, small deep-set openings, planted courtyards — and each tier above becomes lighter and more open than the one below it: masonry gives way to steel frame, then to full-height glazing, then to an open pergola terrace at the top with only posts and a thin roof plane. The progression is the argument of the board, so the modules stay aligned in the same footprint and only their weight and openness change. Thin leader lines run from three tiers to short annotations at the right margin, and a small elevation diagram sits at the foot. Warm white ground, concrete grey, one moss green accent in the planting. A line of type at the top reads STACK SERIES and the three annotations are the only other text.",
