      "prompt": "The strongest part is not the watercolor style. It is the facade being reduced into one memorable architectural symbol.\nThis kind of sales poster works when the building can be read in three seconds: white sculptural shell, blue recessed voids, warm ground-floor glow.\n🧊 continuous soft-edged white architectural envelope wrapping the facade as one carved mass\nThis keeps the elevation from becoming a collection of floors. The viewer sees one unified silhouette first, then discovers the openings and terraces inside it.\n🌊 muted cobalt-blue recessed wall planes with warm amber interiors behind refined glazing\nThis solves the depth problem. The blue voids make the white shell feel dimensional, while the amber ground floor makes the project feel inhabitable and premium.\n\nGPT- image 2 prompt👇\nCreate an ultra-premium architectural sales poster for a fictional luxury real estate developer, preserving the poetic watercolor-and-architectural-sketch language while elevating it into a more international, flagship, and symbolically distilled presentation. The composition is a clean vertical single-building facade study on a warm off-white paper background, with one boutique mixed-use landmark centered in the frame and surrounded by only a minimal amount of elegant technical notation. The image must feel like a collectible developer launch sheet, a top-tier architectural watercolor presentation, and a luxury property campaign visual at the same time.\n\nDesign one slender urban boutique building with a highly recognizable sculptural facade: a continuous soft-edged white architectural envelope wraps the whole elevation like a single flowing carved mass, forming two or three large rounded voids that reveal deep blue recessed wall planes and warm interior spaces. The facade silhouette must be instantly memorable and extremely clean, reading as one unified super symbol. The ground floor should be transparent, inviting, and premium, with warm amber lighting, refined glazing, elegant timber frames, and subtle hospitality or sales-gallery atmosphere. Upper levels should feel quieter and more exclusive, with selective planted terraces or inset lounge spaces. Add one strategically placed tree growing through or in front of a facade opening to soften the composition and enhance lifestyle appeal, but keep it subordinate to the building form.\n\nPush the Transit direction strongly: simplify the page, reduce annotation density, enlarge negative space, clean up all secondary details, and make the whole image feel more international, luxurious, editorial, and controlled. Push the Port direction strongly: compress the visual memory into three dominant symbols only, the central white sculptural facade mass, the deep blue recessed voids, and the warm glowing ground-floor interface. Everything else must become light atmosphere. The building must remain the unquestioned visual hero.\n\nPreserve the watercolor effect clearly and elegantly: soft pigment washes, transparent tonal layering, restrained ink outlines, delicate pencil construction traces, subtle drafting notes, light paper grain, and controlled pigment bloom around edges and shadows. The rendering must not look like a loose illustration; it should feel like a highly disciplined architect’s hand-rendered presentation where watercolor atmosphere and real material logic coexist perfectly. Materials must still read believably: matte white stucco or lime plaster, muted cobalt-blue inset planes, warm natural wood, low-iron glass, pale stone paving, and fresh green foliage.\n\nLighting should be soft, luminous, and premium, like late afternoon daylight translated through watercolor media. Use gentle warm-cool contrast: creamy white shell, slightly cooler blue recesses, warm interior amber glow, and tender green foliage catching light. Shadows should remain transparent and watercolor-like, never heavy, but deep enough to give the architecture volume and presence. Keep the street calm and minimal, with only a tiny human scale figure if needed, very secondary.\n\nGraphic layout must be extremely restrained: one minimal fictional developer mark, one elegant original English project title, and a few sparse English architectural notes placed around the facade with beautiful spacing. Do not copy any source wording. Typography should feel premium, architectural, small, and secondary to the building image. The whole page should look quieter, more expensive, and more iconic than a typical facade study.\n\nColor system: warm off-white paper, ivory-white facade, muted cobalt-blue recessed spaces, amber interior light, soft timber brown, pale graphite linework, and restrained spring green foliage. Keep saturation disciplined, breathable, and globally premium. The final image must feel calm, sophisticated, highly brandable, and immediately recognizable.\n\nNo copied brand names, no copied cafe text, no heavy black shadows, no messy sketch clutter, no distorted architecture, no fantasy structure, no overcrowded street scene, no random cars, no excessive people, no dirty paper stains, no black blotches, no watermark, no existing logos.",
      "prompt_ko": "★★**작성자의 한 줄** — *「가장 강한 부분은 수채 스타일이 아니다. ★파사드가 «하나의 기억되는 건축 제스처»로 축약된 것이다」*.\n\n★**나머지를 «일부러 약하게» 두라는 지시가 함께 있어야 성립한다** — 벽은 옅은 워시, 주변은 나무 두세 획과 물의 젖은 선 하나. 그래야 «들어 올린 지붕 곡선» 하나만 남는다.\n\n★화풍(수채)이 아니라 «무엇을 남기고 무엇을 지울지»가 이 항목의 지식이다 — 오늘 여러 번 확인한 「하나만 강조하려면 나머지를 규칙으로 눌러야 한다」와 같다.",
      "gen_prompt": "An architectural sales poster in a poetic watercolour-and-ink manner, in which the whole building is reduced to one memorable architectural gesture: a long horizontal roof plane that lifts at one end into a slow curve, like a sheet of paper caught by wind, carried on slender columns above a glass ground floor. Everything else is deliberately understated — the walls are pale washes, the surroundings are two or three ink strokes for trees and a wet line for the water below — so that single curve is the only thing the eye keeps. Loose transparent washes with visible blooms, bare paper for the sky, fine ink lines only along the roof edge and the column bases. A short line of type at the lower left reads LIFT HOUSE and a small annotation sits beneath it. Those two are the only text in the image.",
