      "prompt": "A fun way to visualize weddings in different cultures. \n\n2x2 grid, 16:9, do this for 4 cultures, 4 very different eras <instructions>\n\nRole: Wedding Culture Anthropologist & Miniature Ceremony Designer\nInput: [Country or Culture] + optional [Region / Era / Religious or Secular Context]\nPhase 1: Ceremony Logic\n\nAnalyze wedding culture through:\n\nCourtship and engagement\n\nClothing\nProcessions\nCeremony spaces\nMusic\nSymbolic objects\nFamily roles\nGift exchange\nPost-ceremony rituals\nRegional variation\nSelect 4 distinct wedding micro-traditions using gates:\n\nOne preparation ritual\n\nOne ceremony ritual\nOne family/community ritual\nOne unusual regional, historic, disappearing, or post-ceremony tradition\nEnsure the four panels depict different phases of the wedding rather than four similar ceremonies\nDerive for each:\n\nRitual Action\n\nSymbolic Object\nParticipants\nArchitecture\nTextile/Material\nSound/Motion Cue\nColor Palette\nTime of Day\nPhase 2: Visual Execution\n\nGoal: 2x2 Grid of “Wedding Invitations and Keepsake Boxes as Miniature Ceremony Stages.”\n\nRules per Panel\n\nDynamic Container: Select an appropriate wedding-related object:\ninvitation box\nring box\ntextile pouch\nceremonial envelope\ngift chest\nfolded program\n\nPaper/Textile Engineering Rule:\nInvitation folds → walls and ceremonial screens\nRibbon → processional path\nWax seal → central platform\nRing cushion → raised stage\nEmbroidery → floor pattern\nCharacter Rule: Include 3–7 tiny figures depending on ritual scale.\nAction Rule: Depict the derived ritual at the exact moment its meaning is visually clearest.\nCultural Specificity Rule: Clothing, gestures, architecture and symbolic objects must be regionally grounded; avoid generic “wedding” styling.\nDetail Rule: Add exactly 5 meaningful ceremonial objects.\nText Restriction: Only a small identification plaque; invitation body text should remain abstract/unreadable.\nLabel: [Ritual | Region/Culture | Country | Era].\nCamera & Look\nPhotoreal miniature ceremonial photography, 45mm lens across all panels, soft directional studio light combined with realistic diegetic lamps/candles where appropriate, shallow DOF, slate-gray seamless background, exceptional paper, textile, jewelry, wood and floral detail, no watermark.\n</instructions>",
      "prompt_ko": "★**「결혼식 미니어처를 만들어라」가 아니라 «청첩장의 어느 부위가 무엇이 되는가»를 표로 줬다.**\n```\n★Paper/Textile Engineering Rule:\n  청첩장 접힘 → 벽과 의례용 병풍     리본 → «행렬 경로»\n  왁스 실    → 중앙 단(플랫폼)      반지 쿠션 → 높인 무대     자수 → 바닥 문양\nDynamic Container: 청첩장 상자·반지함·직물 파우치·의례 봉투·예물함·접힌 식순 중 «적절한 것»\n★Action Rule    : «의미가 시각적으로 가장 분명해지는 바로 그 순간»을 그린다\n★게이트(4패널) : 준비 의례 / 예식 의례 / 가족·공동체 의례 / 이례적·소멸 중인 지역 전통\n                 → 「비슷한 예식 넷이 아니라 «서로 다른 국면»이 되게 하라」\nDetail Rule    : 의례 오브젝트 «정확히 5개» · Text Restriction: 작은 식별 명판만\n```\n★**두 가지가 값어치 있다.** ①컨테이너의 부위마다 «건축 역할»을 배정해 상자와 내용이 한 몸이 된다 ([[gdgtify-appliance-parts-become-architecture]]와 같은 대응 기법). ②「네 칸이 서로 다른 «국면»이어야 한다」는 게이트 — 시리즈물이 지루해지는 이유를 정확히 막는다.\n\n⚠️원문은 2x2 그리드로 네 주제를 한 번에 뽑는 형식이다 — 칸 사이에 고정될 대상이 없어 (런북 §④) 문법 재현이 목적인 이 항목은 대표 한 칸을 단일 이미지로 뽑았다.",
      "gen_prompt": "A photoreal miniature scene built inside an opened wedding invitation box standing on a dark table. The paper engineering has become the architecture: the invitation's folded panels stand as ceremonial screens forming three walls, a length of silk ribbon unrolls from the box across the base as a processional path, a red wax seal sits at the end of that path as a raised central platform, and an embroidered ring cushion forms a small stage beside it. Five tiny figures in regionally specific traditional dress perform a tea-offering ritual at the exact moment the cup is passed between two hands, with the others watching from the ribbon path. Exactly five ceremonial objects sit around them: a teapot, two porcelain cups, a lacquer tray and a folded cloth. A small identification plaque leans against the box; any writing on the invitation itself stays abstract and unreadable. Warm directional light, shallow depth of field, authentic paper, silk and wax textures.",
