      "prompt": "The wildlife shot feels premium because the bird is framed like a rare field encounter, not a decorative mascot.\n🪽 the full peafowl contained inside three tall vertical viewing panels\nThis gives the image a documentary restraint. You see the bird in fragments, but it still reads as one real animal observed through a controlled set of windows.\n🌿 soft misty forest behind the panels, subdued enough to support the subject\nThis is what gives it that BBC-style nature-photography mood. The forest adds habitat, scale, and atmosphere, while the peafowl remains the sharp living jewel in front.\n\nGPT- image 2 prompt👇\nCreate a premium vertical wildlife campaign poster for a fictional eco-travel and conservation brand called VERDANT REALM, combining Orbit-style visual rhythm with Transit-style editorial restraint. The image must feel rare, calm, graphic, and internationally premium. The green peafowl must remain the absolute visual hero, and the entire bird must be visually contained within three tall vertical cutout frames. The peafowl must not extend outside these three vertical panels.\n\nCore composition:\nUse a warm off-white minimalist background in the upper half and a misty forest landscape in the lower half. At the center of the poster, place three tall vertical rectangular crop panels standing side by side with even spacing. The realistic adult green peafowl is revealed only inside these three panels, as if the subject exists entirely within a triptych of vertical viewing windows. No part of the peafowl should break outside the panel edges. The bird should still read as one coherent full-body subject across the three frames, with the segmentation creating a premium editorial rhythm rather than visual fragmentation.\n\nSubject placement:\nThe peafowl stands upright on a weathered wooden post, centered across the three panels. The head and upper neck appear in the left and middle panel relationship, the torso and legs align mainly through the center panel, and the folded tail descends through the right panel. The bird must feel precisely composed so the three windows create a clean visual cadence from head to body to tail. The pose is poised, elegant, alert, and noble, facing slightly left, with correct anatomy and natural balance.\n\nOrbit direction:\nStrengthen the visual flow through the triptych segmentation. The three panels should guide the eye vertically and sequentially, creating a subtle scanning rhythm from crest and head, through the iridescent neck and body, down to the tail and perch. The composition should feel like a controlled visual orbit contained within the three frames, where each panel carries one phase of the bird’s presence. The internal feather direction, leg placement, and perch alignment should reinforce this elegant progression.\n\nTransit restraint:\nKeep the layout highly disciplined and minimal. Reduce all nonessential text and decoration. Preserve large breathing room around the central three-panel structure. Let the poster read instantly as: logo, title, three vertical frames, rare peafowl, mist forest. No extra graphic clutter, no unnecessary overlays, no busy background detail.\n\nBird realism:\nUse a highly realistic adult green peafowl with deep teal and emerald iridescent neck feathers, bronze-brown scaled body plumage, a refined crest, slender pale legs, natural claws gripping the post, and a long folded tail. Anatomy must be exact and believable: correct beak shape, clean neck curve, realistic leg joints, proper feather layering, and natural posture. No tail fan display, no fantasy exaggeration.\n\nBackground:\nThe lower portion behind the three panels shows a lush forested hillside fading into soft mist. The misty forest should remain subdued and atmospheric, supporting the subject without competing with it. The top portion stays mostly clean and pale, reinforcing luxury poster minimalism.\n\nLighting:\nSoft diffused natural daylight with gentle highlight definition on the head, neck, and upper torso. The iridescent feathers should show subtle emerald-blue shift without artificial metallic glare. The forest below should feel softly illuminated through mist. Contrast must be elegant and precise, with no harsh shadows and no dead black areas.\n\nMaterial and texture:\nUltra-detailed feather structure, realistic iridescent neck sheen, fine crest texture, natural leg skin, believable claws, and lightly weathered wooden post grain. Forest foliage should show layered tonal depth and mist softness. The poster surface should feel clean, refined, and premium.\n\nTypography and branding:\nAt the top center, place a small refined logo for VERDANT REALM. Beneath it, add a premium English title: “RARE IN SILENCE”. Add one very small supporting line only: “A living jewel within the canopy.” Keep typography sparse, elegant, and secondary to the three-panel peafowl composition, in muted olive-gold or soft charcoal.\n\nColor system:\nWarm off-white, mist grey, forest green, deep emerald, teal-blue iridescence, muted bronze-brown plumage, and natural wood beige. Keep saturation restrained and premium.\n\nStyle:\nCannes-level wildlife campaign poster, luxury documentary-editorial branding, minimalist composition fused with strong graphic framing language, collectible, calm, rare, and globally marketable.\n\nNegative prompt:\nbird extending outside the three panels, broken triptych layout, copied original text, copied original logo, cartoon bird, fantasy peacock, exaggerated tail display, broken anatomy, extra legs, distorted claws, warped neck, messy segmentation, cluttered poster, oversaturated feathers, fake CGI iridescence, muddy mist, unreadable typography, watermark, low-end travel poster",
