      "title": "★네 장이 «구조는 공유하고 운동은 달리한다» — 자리를 고정하고 움직임만 바꾼다",
      "prompt": "The system works because the four posters share one structure, but not one motion.\n🪽 same logo block, circle field, info block, and date position across all four posters\nThis makes the campaign feel like one identity. You see consistency before you compare the dancers, so the board reads as a designed festival system instead of four separate images.\n✨ leap, drift, turn, rise as a directional sequence across the 2x2 grid\nThis gives the layout movement beyond each individual pose. The eye travels from card to card, and the dancers start to feel like one continuous choreography.\n\nGPT- image 2 prompt👇\nCreate a premium 2x2 campaign board for a fictional contemporary dance festival brand called AERIA MOTION, combining Orbit-style directional movement with Transit-style editorial restraint. Present four vertically oriented dance posters arranged in a precise grid on a pale neutral-grey presentation background. The full system must feel like one unified international cultural identity: clean, intelligent, collectible, and visually fluid.\n\nOverall board layout:\nA 2x2 grid of four evenly spaced posters on a soft light-grey field. Each poster is printed on bright matte white stock with subtle rounded corners and identical proportions. The campaign must read as one coherent visual system, not four separate ads. The spacing, margins, typography, and structural hierarchy remain consistent across all four posters.\n\nCore poster system:\nEach poster contains:\n1. a small black geometric logo and festival identity block at the upper-left,\n2. one realistic adult dancer as the absolute hero,\n3. one large circular visual field behind or intersecting the dancer,\n4. one minimal informational text block in the lower-middle,\n5. one clean large date line at the bottom.\nThe structure stays constant; only the dancer, movement direction, costume, and circular tone vary.\n\nOrbit movement:\nThe four dancers must form a flowing directional sequence across the entire 2x2 board, so the eye travels naturally from poster to poster. Each body pose should imply a different vector of movement:\nPoster 1: a female dancer in pale aqua translucent fabric, captured in a suspended sideways leap, body opening left-to-right across a soft turquoise circular field.\nPoster 2: a female dancer in fitted white, gliding diagonally upward with one arm reaching, paired with a deep midnight circular field.\nPoster 3: a female dancer in ivory or blush layered tulle, folded into a low turning motion, arms extended laterally, anchored by a dark navy circle.\nPoster 4: a female dancer in bold crimson flowing fabric, rising vertically with one arm lifted and one leg grounded, intersecting a pale aqua atmospheric circle.\nAcross the full board, the poses should create a quiet but powerful orbit-like visual rhythm: leap, drift, turn, rise.\n\nTransit restraint:\nKeep the campaign highly minimal and highly controlled. Typography is sparse, breathing room is generous, and no decorative clutter is allowed. The white paper field around each dancer should remain dominant. The circles must be clean and calm, not loud graphic effects. The board should feel like a museum-quality contemporary dance identity system.\n\nDancer realism:\nUse four realistic adult dancers with correct anatomy, elegant body proportions, expressive hands, five natural fingers, believable feet and ankle articulation, realistic shoulder and neck transitions, and subtle natural skin texture. No distortions, no broken limbs, no exaggerated fashion anatomy. Fabric must respond naturally to movement and gravity. Hair motion must feel physically plausible and graceful.\n\nCircular field treatment:\nEach poster contains one strong circular field placed behind the dancer’s torso or motion path. The circles vary in mood but stay within one family: watery aqua mist, deep midnight blue, dark atmospheric navy, soft sea-glass teal. These circles function as visual anchors and rhythmic counterpoints, not as busy backgrounds.\n\nTypography and branding:\nUse original English-only typography.\nTop-left:\nA small abstract logo mark\n“AERIA MOTION”\n“CONTEMPORARY DANCE FORUM”\nLower-middle:\nvery small venue/program/location text in restrained sans-serif\nBottom:\none large elegant date line, for example “18 / 09 / 2027”\nTypography should be sparse, Swiss-inspired, and perfectly aligned, in black or soft charcoal, with strong hierarchy and ample whitespace.\n\nLighting:\nBright clean studio lighting on each dancer with soft directional shaping and minimal elegant shadow. The figures should appear luminous and crisp against the matte white poster field. The circles may hold slight atmospheric depth, but the dancers remain the brightest visual anchors. The board presentation background stays neutral and unobtrusive.\n\nMaterial and finish:\nUltra-real dancers, realistic skin detail, precise fabric translucency, lightweight textile motion, fine hair strands, premium matte poster stock, and a clean gallery-style mounting presentation. No cheap flyer feeling, no glossy commercial noise, no cluttered paper texture.\n\nColor system:\nWhite, black, pale grey, aqua, midnight blue, navy, ivory, blush, and one controlled crimson accent. The full system must feel elegant, cultural, and internationally premium.\n\nStyle:\nCannes-level cultural poster campaign, premium contemporary dance branding, editorial minimalism, Swiss layout discipline, fashion-photography realism, collectible visual identity, globally marketable.\n\nNegative prompt:\ncluttered layout, copied text, copied logo, bad anatomy, extra fingers, missing fingers, fused fingers, distorted limbs, broken feet, awkward dancer pose, cheap flyer design, messy typography, oversaturated colors, low-detail fabric, cartoon dancer, illustration, painting, random background clutter, warped poster proportions, watermark",
      "prompt_ko": "> *\"The system works because the four posters **share one structure, but not one motion.**\"*\n```\n★same logo block, circle field, info block, and date position across all four\n   ★로고 블록 · 원형 영역 · 정보 블록 · 날짜의 «자리»는 네 장 모두 동일\n```\n★**시리즈를 만드는 두 축이 여기서 갈린다.** 전부 같으면 지루하고 전부 다르면 흩어지는데, **「자리는 고정, 내용의 움직임은 자유」**로 나누면 한 시스템으로 읽히면서 각 장이 살아난다.\n\n★우리 쪽 대응 항목: [[reference-series-lock-by-img2img]]가 «입력 이미지»로 시리즈를 잠근다면, 이건 «레이아웃 슬롯»으로 잠근다. 텍스트만으로 시리즈를 묶어야 할 때 쓸 방법이다.\n\n★@ou_zhen599 계열은 본문 첫 줄이 «왜 이 구성이 성립하는가»를 스스로 적는다 — 그 한 줄이 곧 이 카드의 제목이다.",
      "gen_prompt": "Four square poster panels arranged in a 2x2 grid, all sharing exactly the same layout skeleton: a small solid logo block in the upper left, a large circular field centred in the middle, a two-line information block in the lower left, and a date in the lower right. Those four positions are identical in every panel and never shift. What changes is the motion inside the circle: in the first it is a still flat disc of colour; in the second a swirl of fine lines rotates within it; in the third a burst of particles pushes outward past its edge; in the fourth a soft gradient drifts diagonally through it. Each panel uses one different dominant colour — ochre, teal, crimson, deep violet — against the same bone-white ground. The typography is small, identical in size and weight across all four, and the whole set reads as one system.",
