      "prompt": "I used this prompt for popular jobs of different decades but it works for other topics too. \n\n2x2 grid, 16:9, do this for most popular jobs of 1850, 1940, 1990, 2026      I(0) = blank matte background with a single flat folded page.  I(1) = final isometric pop‑up paper diorama of $ THEME, layered kirigami planes standing up from a central spine, the open spread cut to silhouette $ SHAPE. ∂I/∂t = Δ(I, $ THEME, $ SHAPE) with constraints: isometric projection, back‑to‑front plane order, valley/mountain fold logic, paper‑craft materiality, silhouette adherence, soft paper shadows. Integration: t=0.0–0.15: the flat page and faint crease guides for silhouette $ SHAPE and each fold line appear. t=0.15–0.4: the rearmost backdrop plane (the setting of $ THEME) rises first along the spine — layered cut‑paper skyline or backdrop. t=0.4–0.7: middle planes fold up in sequence, each a cut‑paper scene of $ THEME, tabs and slots visible where layers interlock. t=0.7–0.9: the frontmost pop‑up elements spring forward, completing the depth and the open spread's silhouette. t=0.9–1.0: soft shadows fall between paper layers, small captions appear on tab labels along the spine, final polish. Output I(1) as an 8K pop‑up paper‑diorama masterwork.",
      "prompt_ko": "★**팝업북을 «완성된 모습»이 아니라 «일어서는 순서»로 적는다.**\n```\nconstraints: isometric projection, ★back-to-front plane order,\n             ★valley/mountain fold logic, paper-craft materiality, silhouette adherence\nt=0.15–0.4  가장 «뒤쪽» 배경 면이 척추를 따라 먼저 선다\nt=0.4–0.7   중간 면들이 순서대로 접혀 오르고, ★탭과 슬롯이 보인다\nt=0.7–0.9   맨 «앞» 팝업 요소가 마지막에 선다\n```\n★**「뒤에서 앞으로」라는 순서가 구조를 만든다.** 팝업북을 그리라고만 하면 종이 조각이 흩어진 그림이 나오는데, 면의 순서와 접기 방향(산/계곡), 탭·슬롯의 존재를 지정하면 **실제로 접히는 물건**이 된다.\n\n★같은 날 배치의 [[xiaoxiaodong01-layered-not-dissected]]가 «앵커»로 재조립 가능성을 확보한 것과 통한다 — 이쪽은 «척추»가 그 역할을 한다.",
      "gen_prompt": "An isometric pop-up paper diorama standing open on a plain pale surface, everything cut and folded from matte card. A central spine runs down the middle of the open spread, and the scene rises from it in clearly ordered layers: the rearmost plane is a tall cut-paper skyline of chimneys and pitched roofs; in front of it a middle plane of shopfronts folds up, its awnings scored and bent forward; nearer still a row of market stalls and a handcart stand on their own tabs; and at the very front two small figures are cut free and lean slightly out over the gutter. Interlocking tabs and slots are plainly visible where each layer grips the next, mountain and valley creases catch the light differently, and soft paper shadows fall between the planes. Warm off-white card with three muted ink colours, crisp cut edges, visible fibre.",
